  "sol.gestRistorante.title": "Restaurant back office: floor, kitchen and delivery in one place",
  "sol.gestRistorante.lede":
    "Orders go from the tablet to the kitchen screen, delivery orders join the same flow, and at the end of the night you know what you took and what it cost you.",
  "sol.gestRistorante.problem":
    "On the floor it gets written on a pad, in the kitchen someone has to read the handwriting. The phone takes bookings into a notebook while three delivery tablets buzz on the counter, each in its own format. Whoever is at the stove holds it all together from memory. At the end of the night you know the takings because the till says so, but nobody knows which dish actually made you money.",
  "sol.gestRistorante.signals":
    "Handwritten orders turn into mistakes in the kitchen|Orders arrive from the floor, the phone and three delivery tablets, each in a different format|You do not know which dish earns a margin and which just keeps the oven busy|Bookings live in a notebook and on Saturday someone turns up with no table",
  "sol.gestRistorante.build":
    "From the tablet to the kitchen screen::The waiter enters it at the table and the order appears at the right station, with preparation times. Intolerances come up in red, where they actually need to be seen.|Delivery in the same flow::Platform orders land on the kitchen screen alongside the rest. Nobody has to watch three tablets in turn any more.|Bookings that know the real capacity::The calendar knows the covers and the sittings. When a service is full it stops accepting, instead of accepting and leaving someone standing.|A menu that switches itself off::A dish that runs out is disabled on the floor and on the digital menu at the same moment, so at half past ten nobody orders it.|Food cost per dish::Every order draws the ingredients from the recipe. At the end of the month you see the margin per dish and per sitting, not just the total.",
  "sol.gestRistorante.excludes":
    "The fiscal till and the transmission of daily takings, which stay with your till and your accountant|Accounting and payroll",
  "sol.gestRistorante.phases":
    "Menu, recipes and stations::1 week::We write down the menu with its recipes, and decide how many stations the kitchen has and who sees what.|Floor and kitchen::4-6 weeks::Orders, kitchen screen and bookings. It gets tested with a fake service on a closed night before it touches a real one.|Delivery and numbers::2-3 weeks::We connect the platforms, the food cost and the end-of-night report.",
  "sol.gestRistorante.integra":
    "Glovo, Deliveroo and JustEat|Your fiscal till|TheFork and bookings from Google|Order printers per station|Card terminals (Nexi, SumUp, Satispay)|Your accounting software",
  "sol.gestRistorante.faq":
    "Do I have to change my till?::No. The system works alongside the one you have: it passes the bill over, and the fiscal side stays its business.|What if the connection drops mid-service?::Orders keep moving locally and resync when the line comes back. A service cannot depend on the venue wifi.|How long does a waiter take to learn it?::One service, if the keypad is built around your menu rather than a generic one. That is the part we decide together at the start, not afterwards.|Is it worth it for a small place?::Under thirty covers often not, and I will say so up front. With one sitting and two people in the kitchen the pad still works. It earns its keep when orders come from more than one source.",
  "sol.gestRistorante.change":
    "The kitchen receives one legible thing, whichever direction the order came from. And at the end of the month you know which dish keeps you open and which one only keeps the oven busy.",
